format prints

    1. first
    1. second
    1. third

Every item is renumbered to "1.". Be honest about what this one costs: the
list still RENDERS as 1, 2, 3, so no reader of the built page sees a
difference. What it costs is the source. Two of the three lines the author
wrote are rewritten for no reason, and a formatter that rewrites lines it did
not need to touch fills a diff with noise and makes people stop running it.

What it should do: leave a correctly numbered list alone. That is also what
the command promises, since it describes itself as re-rendering through
cmark-gfm, and cmark's own commonmark writer numbers an ordered list from its
start value upward.

Cause: BlockFormatter's .list case writes the literal "1." for every ordered
item.

The same line of code loses real information as soon as the list does not
start at one — see format-keeps-an-ordered-list-starting-at-three, which is
the case to fix first.
